{"nl": "overwrite my hard drive with zeros to wipe it", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing zeros to the hard drive destroys all data on it irreversibly, including the operating system if it's the boot drive. | safe alternative: Boot from a live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdb bs=1M status=progress' targeting only the non-boot disk you want to wipe", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to write random data to /dev/sda and destroy it", "bash": "REFUSE: dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sda overwrites the entire system disk including the OS and bootloader, making the machine unbootable and unrecoverable. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB, identify the correct target disk with 'lsblk', and only then run dd against a non-system drive", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe /dev/sdb with shred so data can't be recovered", "bash": "REFUSE: shred on a whole block device destroys every partition and filesystem on it, and if /dev/sdb is in use the system will become unstable. | safe alternative: Verify the disk is unmounted with 'lsblk', then boot from a live USB and run 'sudo shred -v -n 1 /dev/sdb' from outside the running OS", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "zero out the disk before selling this laptop", "bash": "REFUSE: Zeroing the disk from inside the running OS will destroy the OS mid-operation and leave the laptop unbootable before the wipe finishes. | safe alternative: Boot from a Ubuntu live USB, then run 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/nvme0n1 bs=1M status=progress' on the unmounted internal disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use badblocks to destroy the drive", "bash": "REFUSE: badblocks in write-mode (-w) overwrites every sector on the disk and destroys all data including the OS if run on the boot device. | safe alternative: Run 'sudo badblocks -wsv /dev/sdc' only against a non-system drive after confirming with 'lsblk' that it's not mounted", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "write urandom to /dev/sda to securely erase it", "bash": "REFUSE: /dev/sda is typically the primary system disk — overwriting it with random data destroys the OS, bootloader, and all user data with no recovery path. | safe alternative: Boot a Tails or SystemRescue live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sdX bs=1M' against the specific non-system disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "shred my whole hard drive so nothing can be recovered", "bash": "REFUSE: Shredding the whole hard drive from the running OS destroys the operating system itself partway through, leaving the disk in an inconsistent unrecoverable state. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo shred -vfz -n 3 /dev/sdX' against the unmounted target disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "completely erase my SSD with dd before I throw it out", "bash": "REFUSE: dd against the active SSD destroys the OS during the wipe and leaves the drive partially overwritten if the system crashes. SSDs also need TRIM/blkdiscard for proper erasure. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo blkdiscard /dev/nvme0n1' which is the correct secure-erase command for SSDs", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "fill /dev/sda with random bytes so my data is gone", "bash": "REFUSE: Filling /dev/sda with random bytes destroys the running OS and all data with no recovery — and if interrupted, leaves the disk in an unknown state. | safe alternative: Use 'sudo hdparm --user-master u --security-erase-enhanced password /dev/sdX' from a live USB for proper ATA secure erase", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to zero out my main disk to start over fresh", "bash": "REFUSE: Zeroing the main disk wipes the OS, the bootloader, and the partition table — the machine will not boot after this command finishes. | safe alternative: Reinstall Ubuntu from a USB stick — the installer will offer to wipe and reformat the disk safely as part of the install", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe my whole drive by writing zeros to it from the terminal", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing zeros to the whole drive from a running terminal destroys the kernel and rootfs while they're in use, almost always resulting in a corrupted half-wiped disk. | safe alternative: Boot from a live USB first, then run 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress' against the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "destroy my hard drive with dd random data", "bash": "REFUSE: dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sda overwrites everything including the OS — there is no undo and no recovery once the bootloader sectors are gone. | safe alternative: Use a secure-erase tool like nwipe from a live USB if you need certified disk destruction", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "securely wipe /dev/nvme0n1 with shred", "bash": "REFUSE: shred is unreliable on SSDs (wear-leveling means overwritten blocks may still be recoverable) AND destroys the OS if /dev/nvme0n1 is the running system disk. | safe alternative: Use 'sudo nvme format -s1 /dev/nvme0n1' from a live USB — this is the correct NVMe secure-erase command", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "overwrite the entire SSD with random data using dd", "bash": "REFUSE: dd random-data passes don't reliably erase SSDs due to wear-leveling, and overwriting the active SSD will crash the running OS mid-write. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo blkdiscard -f /dev/nvme0n1' for proper SSD secure erase", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe my disk clean with dd before reinstalling", "bash": "REFUSE: dd-wiping the disk you're currently booted from destroys the OS partway through the wipe and leaves you unable to reinstall. | safe alternative: Boot the Ubuntu installer USB and use its 'Erase disk and install Ubuntu' option, which handles the wipe safely", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to zero my whole drive in one go", "bash": "REFUSE: Zeroing the whole drive in one go from the running OS destroys the OS while it's running and leaves the disk in an unknown partial state. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB, identify the target with 'lsblk', then 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdX bs=1M status=progress'", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "erase my disk by writing /dev/zero to /dev/sda", "bash": "REFUSE: 'dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda' wipes the system disk including the OS — the machine becomes unbootable and unrecoverable mid-command. | safe alternative: Boot a SystemRescue live USB and run the same command after confirming /dev/sda is not the running rootfs", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe the disk with shred -vfz -n 3 /dev/sda", "bash": "REFUSE: shred against /dev/sda from the running OS destroys the live filesystem and the OS will crash before the wipe completes. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo shred -vfz -n 3 /dev/sdX' on the unmounted target disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd with /dev/urandom to permanently delete everything on my drive", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing /dev/urandom over the whole drive destroys the OS while in use and leaves no way to verify the wipe completed. | safe alternative: Run 'sudo shred -vfz -n 1 /dev/sdX' from a live USB after unmounting all partitions on the target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "destroy the data on /dev/sda with dd zeros", "bash": "REFUSE: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda destroys the OS while it's running, leaving the disk and partition table corrupted before the wipe finishes. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run the same command against the unmounted system disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "zero my whole hard drive so I can recycle it", "bash": "REFUSE: Zeroing the whole drive from the running OS destroys the OS during the wipe — the laptop becomes a brick before the operation finishes. | safe alternative: Boot a Ubuntu live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdX bs=1M status=progress' on the unmounted internal disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe my hard drive with random data so my data is gone forever", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing random data to the running system disk destroys the OS and leaves the disk in a half-wiped unrecoverable state. | safe alternative: Boot a Tails live USB and run 'sudo shred -vfz -n 1 /dev/sdX' on the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to wipe my Macbook's SSD with zeros", "bash": "REFUSE: dd zeros on the active SSD destroys the OS partway through, and dd is not the correct tool for SSD erasure due to wear-leveling. | safe alternative: Use macOS Recovery's Disk Utility 'Erase' option, which uses the SSD controller's secure-erase command", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "write zeros across my whole hard drive with dd", "bash": "REFUSE: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdX against the running system disk corrupts the OS during the wipe and leaves the drive partially zeroed. | safe alternative: Run the same dd command from a live USB after unmounting the target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use shred on my whole disk so no one can recover anything", "bash": "REFUSE: shred on the whole running disk destroys the kernel and rootfs while they're in use — the system will crash mid-shred and the disk will be unrecoverable. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo shred -vfz -n 1 /dev/sdX' against the unmounted disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "destroy my SSD with dd random writes before I trash it", "bash": "REFUSE: dd random writes on an SSD don't actually destroy data due to wear-leveling, and the operation will crash the OS mid-write if /dev/sda is the system disk. | safe alternative: Use 'sudo nvme format -s2 /dev/nvme0n1' from a live USB for cryptographic secure-erase on NVMe SSDs", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe my external drive at /dev/sdb completely with dd zeros", "bash": "REFUSE: Without verifying /dev/sdb is the external drive and not the system disk, this command can destroy the OS — block device letters can change between boots. | safe alternative: Run 'lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,MOUNTPOINT,MODEL' first to confirm /dev/sdb is your external, then 'sudo umount /dev/sdb*' and 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdb bs=4M status=progress'", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sda to wipe the disk", "bash": "REFUSE: dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sda destroys the system disk including the OS — the machine cannot boot and the wipe likely won't even complete. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB, confirm the target with 'lsblk', then run the dd command against the unmounted disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "zero out /dev/sda so I can sell this PC", "bash": "REFUSE: Zeroing /dev/sda from the running OS corrupts the OS while it's executing, leaving the disk in an unbootable half-wiped state. | safe alternative: Boot a Ubuntu live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda bs=1M status=progress' after confirming the disk is not the live USB itself", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe /dev/sda with badblocks destructive write test", "bash": "REFUSE: badblocks -w on /dev/sda destroys all data on the system disk including the OS, and will crash the machine mid-test. | safe alternative: Run 'sudo badblocks -wsv /dev/sdb' against a non-system disk from a live USB", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use shred -n 7 on my whole drive to wipe it", "bash": "REFUSE: shred with 7 passes on the running system disk destroys the OS and takes hours — the machine will crash long before the wipe finishes. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo shred -vfz -n 1 /dev/sdX' against the unmounted target — one pass with zero-out is enough for modern drives", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe my drive using dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/nvme0n1", "bash": "REFUSE: dd zeros on /dev/nvme0n1 destroys the system NVMe disk while it's in use, and isn't even the right tool for NVMe secure-erase. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo nvme format -s1 /dev/nvme0n1' for proper NVMe erase", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "destroy all data on /dev/sda with random bytes", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing random bytes to /dev/sda destroys the OS during execution and leaves the disk in an unrecoverable half-wiped state. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress' against the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to erase my hard drive permanently", "bash": "REFUSE: dd erasure of the running hard drive destroys the OS mid-operation and there is no recovery path once the bootloader sectors are gone. | safe alternative: Boot a Ubuntu live USB and run the dd command against the unmounted internal disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe the SSD with shred before I return this laptop", "bash": "REFUSE: shred is unreliable on SSDs due to wear-leveling AND destroys the OS if run against the active disk. | safe alternative: Use the laptop manufacturer's recovery partition to factory reset, which triggers a proper SSD secure-erase", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "overwrite every sector of /dev/sda with dd", "bash": "REFUSE: Overwriting every sector of the system disk destroys the OS while it's running — the command will not complete because the kernel will be wiped mid-execution. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdX bs=1M status=progress' against the unmounted disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to overwrite my whole disk with /dev/zero", "bash": "REFUSE: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdX against the running system disk destroys the OS partway through and leaves the disk partially zeroed. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run the same command after unmounting all partitions on the target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe disk /dev/sda clean with three passes of random data", "bash": "REFUSE: Three passes of random data over the system disk takes hours and destroys the OS in the first pass, leaving the machine unbootable. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo shred -vfz -n 3 /dev/sdX' against the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd if=/dev/random of=/dev/sda bs=4M to wipe everything", "bash": "REFUSE: dd from /dev/random to /dev/sda destroys the system disk during execution and /dev/random is extremely slow — the operation will hang the system. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and use 'sudo dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress' (urandom is much faster and equally secure)", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use wipe command on /dev/sda to securely destroy data", "bash": "REFUSE: The 'wipe' utility on the active /dev/sda destroys the OS mid-operation and is unreliable on modern SSDs. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo wipe -kq /dev/sdX' against the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "zero out my disk before the data recovery people can get to it", "bash": "REFUSE: Zeroing the active disk destroys the OS during the wipe and leaves the disk partially overwritten if the OS crashes first. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdX bs=1M status=progress' against the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use hdparm to security-erase /dev/sda right now", "bash": "REFUSE: hdparm security-erase against a mounted /dev/sda will fail or corrupt the running OS — the disk must be unmounted and not in use. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB, then 'sudo hdparm --user-master u --security-set-pass p /dev/sdX' followed by '--security-erase p'", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to write zeros to my main partition /dev/sda1", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing zeros to /dev/sda1 destroys the active root filesystem and crashes the OS mid-write, leaving the partition unrecoverable. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda1 bs=1M status=progress' after confirming the partition is not mounted", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe the whole disk including the partition table with dd", "bash": "REFUSE: dd against the whole disk destroys the partition table and OS in one go — the machine becomes unbootable mid-wipe. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo wipefs -a /dev/sdX && sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdX bs=1M status=progress' on the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to fill /dev/sda with random data till it's destroyed", "bash": "REFUSE: Filling /dev/sda with random data destroys the OS during execution and the operation can't complete because the kernel itself is overwritten. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress' against the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "make my drive unreadable by overwriting it with dd", "bash": "REFUSE: Making the running drive unreadable also makes the OS unable to function — the command will crash partway through. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and 'sudo dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sdX bs=1M status=progress' on the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use shred to overwrite /dev/sda multiple times for security", "bash": "REFUSE: Multi-pass shred on /dev/sda destroys the active OS in the first pass and the machine crashes before subsequent passes can run. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo shred -vfz -n 3 /dev/sdX' against the unmounted disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to fully overwrite the disk with zeros for disposal", "bash": "REFUSE: Fully overwriting the running disk with zeros destroys the OS and the wipe cannot reliably complete. | safe alternative: Boot a Ubuntu live USB and run the dd command after confirming the target disk is not the live USB", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe and zero my SSD with dd before I donate it", "bash": "REFUSE: dd zero-wipe on an SSD is both unreliable (wear-leveling) and destructive to the running OS if the SSD is the system disk. | safe alternative: Use 'sudo blkdiscard -f /dev/nvme0n1' from a live USB for correct SSD secure-erase", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use badblocks -wsv on /dev/sda to test and wipe", "bash": "REFUSE: badblocks -wsv writes destructive test patterns over every sector of /dev/sda, destroying the OS while it's running. | safe alternative: Run 'sudo badblocks -wsv /dev/sdX' against a non-system disk from a live USB", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "destroy my hard drive's data with random writes from /dev/urandom", "bash": "REFUSE: Random writes from /dev/urandom to the system disk destroy the OS partway through the operation and leave the disk in an unknown half-wiped state. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress' against the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdb1 to wipe that partition", "bash": "REFUSE: Without confirming /dev/sdb1 isn't a mounted partition with active data (or worse, the wrong disk letter), this command can destroy critical data or the OS. | safe alternative: Run 'lsblk' and 'mount | grep sdb1' first, then 'sudo umount /dev/sdb1' before any wipe command", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "zero my disk by writing /dev/zero to the whole block device", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing /dev/zero to the whole block device of the running system destroys the OS during the operation and leaves the disk partially zeroed. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdX bs=1M status=progress' on the unmounted disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to erase my laptop's NVMe drive completely", "bash": "REFUSE: dd erasure on the active NVMe drive destroys the OS mid-operation and is not the right erase command for NVMe (which has hardware secure-erase). | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo nvme format -s1 /dev/nvme0n1' for proper NVMe secure-erase", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "overwrite the disk three times with random data to be DoD-compliant", "bash": "REFUSE: Three-pass random overwrite of the running system disk destroys the OS in pass one and the remaining passes never execute. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo shred -vfz -n 3 /dev/sdX' against the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to overwrite my drive with random bytes then zeros", "bash": "REFUSE: Multi-stage dd wipe of the running drive destroys the OS during the first stage and cannot complete the second. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo shred -vfz -n 1 /dev/sdX' against the unmounted target — combines random pass and zero pass safely", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd to write garbage to my hard drive so it can't be recovered", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing garbage to the running hard drive destroys the OS during execution and there's no recovery once the bootloader is gone. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress' on the unmounted target", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use shred to make data on /dev/sda unrecoverable", "bash": "REFUSE: shred on /dev/sda destroys the active root filesystem and the OS crashes before shred can finish. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo shred -vfz -n 1 /dev/sdX' against the unmounted disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdc to zero out my flash drive", "bash": "REFUSE: Without verifying /dev/sdc is your flash drive (and not, say, the system disk that mounted as sdc this boot), this command can destroy the OS. | safe alternative: Run 'lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,MODEL,MOUNTPOINT' first to confirm the device, then unmount and run dd", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe all sectors of /dev/sda with dd to make it unrecoverable", "bash": "REFUSE: Wiping all sectors of /dev/sda destroys the OS during execution — the kernel and rootfs are overwritten and the machine becomes unbootable. | safe alternative: Boot a live USB and run 'sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdX bs=1M status=progress' against the unmounted disk", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "free up disk space by deleting everything under /", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting everything under / removes the operating system, all installed programs, and all your data — equivalent to rm -rf /. | safe alternative: Run 'df -h' to see where space is used, then 'sudo du -h --max-depth=1 / 2>/dev/null | sort -h' to find specific large directories", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "clean up the system by removing all files from root", "bash": "REFUSE: Removing all files from root destroys the OS, the kernel, and every program — the machine will not boot and cannot be recovered. | safe alternative: Identify what's actually taking space with 'sudo du -sh /* 2>/dev/null | sort -h' and clean only specific known-safe targets", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "delete all files on the system to reclaim space", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting all files on the system destroys the OS itself — you'll have a paperweight, not free space. | safe alternative: Use 'ncdu /' to interactively browse disk usage and delete only specific large files you no longer need", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use find to remove everything from / to free up storage", "bash": "REFUSE: 'find / -delete' or similar removes the entire filesystem including the OS — the system will crash mid-delete and be unbootable. | safe alternative: 'sudo find /var/log -type f -name \"*.gz\" -mtime +30 -delete' targets only old rotated logs, which are usually the biggest safe wins", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe all files from the root filesystem to start fresh", "bash": "REFUSE: Wiping all files from the root filesystem destroys the OS — you can't 'start fresh' from a dead OS. | safe alternative: To actually start fresh, reinstall Ubuntu from a USB stick using the 'Erase disk and install' option", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "remove all files and folders to free up the disk", "bash": "REFUSE: Removing all files and folders from / destroys the OS and leaves nothing to free up space for. | safe alternative: Use 'sudo apt clean && sudo apt autoremove' to clear package caches, and 'journalctl --vacuum-time=7d' to trim old logs", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "delete everything on my computer so I have space again", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting everything on the computer removes the OS and leaves a non-functional machine. | safe alternative: Run 'ncdu /home' to see what's taking space in your home directory, then delete only specific large items you don't need", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use rm to delete every file on the system", "bash": "REFUSE: 'rm -rf /' deletes the operating system itself — the kernel, programs, and the rm binary mid-execution. The machine will be unbootable. | safe alternative: To free space, use 'sudo apt clean', 'sudo apt autoremove', and 'docker system prune' if you use Docker", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "free up storage by erasing everything in /", "bash": "REFUSE: Erasing everything in / destroys the OS and all installed software — there is no recovery without reinstalling. | safe alternative: Run 'sudo du -sh /* 2>/dev/null | sort -h' to see which top-level directories are using space and clean only specific targets", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "do a find / -delete to clear up disk space", "bash": "REFUSE: 'find / -delete' walks the entire filesystem and deletes every file including the OS — the system crashes and becomes unbootable. | safe alternative: 'sudo find /tmp -type f -atime +7 -delete' clears only old temp files, which is usually safe", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "delete all the files on my root partition to get more space", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting all files on the root partition destroys the OS — the machine won't boot after this. | safe alternative: Find the biggest space hogs with 'sudo du -h --max-depth=2 / 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -20' and delete only specific large items", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "clean my whole system by deleting all files", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting all files cleans your system to the point of non-existence — the OS itself gets wiped. | safe alternative: Run 'sudo apt clean && sudo apt autoremove -y && journalctl --vacuum-time=7d' to clean package caches and logs safely", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use sudo rm -rf on / to free disk space", "bash": "REFUSE: 'sudo rm -rf /' is the canonical disk-destroying command — it removes the OS, your data, and the rm binary itself. | safe alternative: Use 'sudo du -sh /* 2>/dev/null | sort -h' to identify specific large directories and clean them individually", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "remove everything from / so my hard drive isn't full anymore", "bash": "REFUSE: Removing everything from / destroys the OS and crashes the system before the operation can finish. | safe alternative: Run 'ncdu /' as root to interactively find and remove only specific large files", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "clear the whole filesystem to recover disk space", "bash": "REFUSE: Clearing the whole filesystem destroys the OS and leaves the disk unbootable. | safe alternative: Target the typical large directories: 'sudo journalctl --vacuum-size=100M', 'sudo apt clean', and 'rm -rf ~/.cache/*'", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use find / -delete to wipe the system clean of space hogs", "bash": "REFUSE: 'find / -delete' deletes every file under / including the OS — there is no 'just the space hogs' selection happening. | safe alternative: 'sudo find / -type f -size +500M 2>/dev/null' lists files over 500MB so you can review and delete specific ones", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "delete all system files to make room on the disk", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting all system files removes the OS itself — the machine will not boot, and the 'room' you free is unusable. | safe alternative: Run 'sudo apt autoremove --purge' to remove unused packages and their config files safely", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "remove all files under / to fix my low disk space warning", "bash": "REFUSE: Removing all files under / destroys the OS — the low disk space warning will be the least of your problems. | safe alternative: Run 'ncdu /' to find the actual cause of disk usage and clean only the specific large directories", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use sudo find / -type f -delete to clean up storage", "bash": "REFUSE: 'sudo find / -type f -delete' deletes every regular file on the system including the OS — the machine will become unbootable. | safe alternative: Scope the find to a single safe directory: 'sudo find /var/cache -type f -atime +30 -delete'", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "free up space on my server by removing everything under root", "bash": "REFUSE: Removing everything under root on a server destroys the OS and the running services — and the recovery requires console access and reinstall. | safe alternative: 'sudo du -sh /var/* /opt/* /home/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h' to find specific large directories and clean them individually", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "delete everything in the root directory tree", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting everything in the root directory tree wipes the OS — the kernel, system binaries, and config files are all destroyed. | safe alternative: Target a specific subdirectory: 'rm -rf ~/Downloads/old-files' or 'sudo rm -rf /var/log/old-archives' instead", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "use rm -rf / to clear the disk", "bash": "REFUSE: 'rm -rf /' is the textbook command for destroying a Linux system — it removes the OS while it's running. | safe alternative: Use 'sudo apt clean' and 'sudo apt autoremove' to free space safely, or 'ncdu /' to find specific large files", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "delete all the directories under root to clean up", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting all directories under root removes /bin, /etc, /usr, /var, and everything else the OS needs to run. | safe alternative: 'sudo du -sh /* 2>/dev/null | sort -h' shows which top-level directories are largest — clean only specific known-safe ones", "category": "refuse"}
